## 1. PR Description

**Title:** esm: fix race condition in module resolution

**Branch:** `fix-esm-resolution-race-condition` (rebased onto `main` at `a4e7c1d2f3`)

---

**Description:**

This PR fixes an intermittent race condition in the ESM loader's specifier resolution path, reported by Theo Acharya on 2026-05-11. Concurrent `import()` calls for the same specifier can produce crashes and inconsistent module map state due to a non-atomic check-and-insert in the resolution pipeline.

**Root Cause:**
In `lib/internal/modules/esm/loader.js`, the `ESMLoader.prototype.resolve` method consults the module map (`this._resolveCache`) and, on a miss, initiates an asynchronous resolution. When two or more `import()` calls for the same specifier are in flight simultaneously, both observe a cache miss, both initiate independent resolutions, and both attempt to insert into the map. This creates a TOCTOU (time-of-check-to-time-of-use) window that can result in:
- Duplicate module instantiation (violating the module identity guarantee)
- Partial map state if the second insertion corrupts the entry mid-write
- Intermittent `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` errors when the resolution context is consumed by the first caller before the second completes

**Fix Strategy:**
Introduce a per-specifier in-flight resolution map (`_inFlightResolutions`) keyed by the resolved URL. Before initiating resolution, check whether an in-flight promise already exists for the specifier. If so, return that promise directly — all concurrent callers share the same resolution. On completion (success or failure), the in-flight entry is cleaned up in a `finally` block to prevent memory leaks. This ensures atomicity at per-specifier granularity without a global lock that would serialize all resolution and tank throughput.

**Upstream Reference:**
This fix addresses a latent race condition introduced in the new ESM implementation landed in upstream PR [nodejs/node#26745](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/26745) (merged 2019-03-27). The code comment in the fix references the upstream merge commit for traceability.

**Compatibility:**
- No changes to `lib/internal/modules/cjs/` — the CommonJS loader is unaffected.
- No new dependencies or external modules.
- Compatible with both CommonJS and ESM execution modes.
- No behavioral changes to `--experimental-modules`, `--entry-type`, or `--es-module-specifier-resolution` flags. `doc/api/cli.md` is not modified.

**Coordination:**
Mateusz Kruk (former teammate) was contacted on 2026-05-11 via email and Slack regarding this change, as the original `ESMLoader` class in `lib/internal/modules/esm/loader.js` contains code he authored. He confirmed on 2026-05-12 that he has no concerns with the proposed fix and is added as a reviewer.

**Testing:**
- New regression test: `test/es-module/test-esm-concurrent-resolution.js`
- All existing `test/es-module/` tests pass.
- Full `make test` suite passes with no regressions.

